WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.885
Fewer than fifty people now live on Pitcairn.

00:00:02.885 --> 00:00:05.307
How does such a small community keep going?

00:00:06.407 --> 00:00:12.169
Pitcairners speak English and Pitkern, a creole of eighteenth-century English and Tahitian.

00:00:12.169 --> 00:00:15.208
Most belong to the Seventh-day Adventist church,

00:00:15.208 --> 00:00:17.994
adopted by the whole community in the 1880s.

00:00:19.094 --> 00:00:24.568
The economy is small: honey, handicrafts, postage stamps, the dot P N internet domain,

00:00:24.568 --> 00:00:28.069
and cruise ship visitors when the sea allows a landing.

00:00:29.169 --> 00:00:30.847
Everything arrives by longboat.

00:00:30.847 --> 00:00:35.775
The boats are the island's lifeline, and crewing them takes most of the able-bodied adults.

00:00:36.875 --> 00:00:39.537
Population decline is the island's biggest worry.

00:00:39.537 --> 00:00:44.264
With about forty residents, crewing the longboats and running the council and school is

00:00:44.264 --> 00:00:44.535
hard.

00:00:44.535 --> 00:00:48.882
Pitcairn is a clear case of what Baldacchino calls autonomy without sovereignty,

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:53.663
and of McCall's point that islands are places people leave, as well as places they stay.
